A case value must be loaded SIGNED.

    case -2:      ->      :_SWITCH_CASE_4294967294_f_0

The case list stores each value as its LABEL SPELLING, which is rendered
unsigned, and the jump table then recovered the constant by strtoint()ing that
name back. The sign was laundered out through a string. The switch value sits
in the other register sign-extended to the full width, so

    0x00000000FFFFFFFE  !=  0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE

and no negative case ever matched. They fell through to default, or to nothing.

IT PASSED ON amd64, WHICH IS WHY IT LASTED. x86-64's `mov_rax,%imm32`
SIGN-EXTENDS, so the wrong constant landed on the right value and every
negative switch worked by accident. aarch64 builds its immediates
zero-extended and does not. That is the fifth instance of the
invisible-on-amd64/fatal-on-aarch64 class MICRO-C.md counts four of, and it is
the first one the case suite caught by itself -- case 16 has been red on the
aarch64 column and green on amd64 for some time, with only the amd64 column
being read.

NEGATION WITHOUT A NEGATE, which is the only interesting part of the fix.
There is no general negative-immediate lowering in this compiler. The aarch64
table carries exactly three -- `mov_x0,-1`, `-8` and `-100`, the ones M2libc
happened to need -- so `mov x0, -2` would need a new M1 macro on every
architecture, and MICRO-C.md already records per-architecture emission as the
reason bitfield ACCESS is not implemented. It is not necessary here:

    -v  ==  ~(v - 1)

so the magnitude minus one is loaded and bitwise-NOT'd. Every one of those NOT
forms is already emitted for unary `~`, and is therefore already proven against
each architecture's M1 vocabulary. NO NEW MACRO ANYWHERE, on any of the six.

`-(value + 1)` rather than `-value - 1` deliberately: the first is exactly
|value| - 1 and stays representable at INT_MIN, where negating first would
overflow. `case -1:` loads 0 and NOTs it, which is the boundary case and is
tested.

WHY int2str's SPELLING IS LEFT ALONE. Making the label read `-2` would put a
`-` in an M1 symbol name, and rendering it 64-bit would depend on how wide the
HOST's int is -- micro-c's own `int` is 32-bit under gcc and 64-bit once
self-hosted, so the same source would emit different label names depending on
which compiler built the compiler. The name is only a name. What was broken was
using it as a number, and the value now travels beside it as one.

MEASURED, both architectures, patched tables:

    amd64     pass 50  fail 0    unchanged
    aarch64   pass 50  fail 0    16 and 51 closed -- THE aarch64 COLUMN IS
                                 CLEAN FOR THE FIRST TIME
    regression.sh                0 regressions
    tcc                          still builds, 372,475 lines, 1.53 MB, and the
                                 marker trail is unchanged

AND SWITCHES WITHOUT A NEGATIVE CASE EMIT BYTE-IDENTICAL OUTPUT -- checked by
diffing the emitted .M1 for a corpus of ordinary switches (fallthrough,
default, char labels, values past 16 bits) against the pre-patch compiler. The
positive path does not go near the new code.

